1
00:00:00,520 --> 00:00:13,440
Hey everyone! We talked a good bit about uncertainty in a prior video and looked at when to pursue projects based on expected values. Let's keep in-line with that and now look at uncertainty and pricing.

2
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,760
To do so we will keep things simple by examining two types of consumers, though we can extend this beyond two.

3
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:30,440
We have a low value consumer and a high value consumer. The difference between the two is what they are willing to pay for the same item.

4
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,200
Here we are going to look at tacos because it's Tuesday when I'm making this.

5
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:48,160
We have Mr. Low and Ms High who both have a craving for tacos on the fabulous Taco Tuesday. They both like to go to the same restaurant—Tacorama—to enjoy these tacos.

6
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:58,480
The current deal is 3 tacos for $10. While Mr. Low does enjoy tacos, Low only wants to pay $6 for three tacos.

7
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:08,200
Ms. High enjoys tacos so much that she is willing to pay $10 for the tacos—her exact valuation. This leaves a bit of a conundrum for Tacorama.

8
00:01:09,240 --> 00:01:25,120
This leaves a bit of a conundrum for Tacorama. How can they benefit from these transactions? They have two options. The first is to price the tacos at $6 and sell to both in exchange for a combined $12.

9
00:01:25,640 --> 00:01:42,240
In this case, Tacorama is ensuring 100% certainty that both Mr. Low and Ms. High are going to buy the tacos. However, keep in mind that Tacorama incurs $3 in costs to both consumers.

10
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:45,000
Therefore we get a $6 total

11
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:45,840
profit.

12
00:01:46,720 --> 00:01:50,080
If Tacorama instead prices high,

13
00:01:50,600 --> 00:01:59,040
then only Ms. High buys at a price of $10. This leaves Tacorama with $7 in profit.

14
00:01:59,760 --> 00:02:08,200
If Tacorama is trying to maximize its profit, then Tacorama should only price high. However, there is one thing we have forgotten about.

15
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:14,400
What if we could sell to each consumer at their willingness to pay?

16
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:27,040
This brings us to price discrimination. If Tacorama can sell to Mr. Low at $6 and Ms. High at $10, then they'll earn an extra $3 in profit over their next best alternative.

17
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:35,400
To do this does require a bit of work and understanding your consumer base. Let's first consider the case of arbitrage.

18
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:46,400
If Mr. Low can buy tacos at a lower price and sell them to Ms. High at a higher price, then Tacorama misses out on Ms. High's

19
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:48,080
higher valuation.

20
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:58,600
This is kind of like what Uber Eats and Doordash do. They serve as a middleman taking advantage of your higher valuation to stay at home as opposed to going to the restaurant.

21
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:15,320
The fees capture this. Next, if Ms. High learns of Tacorama offering lower prices, she'll of course want to purchase tacos at that lower price. What can Tacorama do?Tacorama can pursue a few strategies, and this isn't an exhaustive list.

22
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:32,560
First, they can offer the cheaper deal earlier in the day. Perhaps, Mr. Low is ok with eating early and Ms. High is not. This may prevent arbitrage and Ms. High seeking the lower price.  Tacorama may offer coupon or punch cards to entice Mr. Low into the restaurant.

23
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:43,880
This does create a problem of incentivizing Ms. High to use the coupon, so the restaurant would need to determine the discount that causes a coupon throwaway to the trash.

24
00:03:44,480 --> 00:04:19,240
Tacorama may offer loyalty rewards to Ms. High if she continues to purchase at the higher price. Though the design of this type of program needs to dissuade Mr. Low from joining. Lastly, Tacorama can bundle the tacos with a drink such that a lower margin item (the tacos) are paired with a higher margin item (the drink) in an effort to recoup lost margins from the tacos. We'll talk more about these strategies and others later in the course.Alright, that's it. Hope you've enjoyed it and we'll catch you in the next one.